ef 17592186045417] [17592186100887 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100887 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186529167 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100887 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186529192 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100887 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying the Graph of an Original Function Given a Translated Function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100887 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186100712 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100887 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "d982b565-2c41-5687-8d00-86b971bfb18b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101091 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186101638 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101091 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186101639 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101091 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186101640 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101091 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186101641 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101091 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186101704 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101091 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186101705 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101091 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101091 #edb/ref 1045 "Vertical Translations of Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101091 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186101638 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101091 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050647 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101091 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101091 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "fa3b0123-3cb4-59d6-8bc7-e2be683f501b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101355 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186099339 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101355 #edb/ref 1069 "q-26123" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101355 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101355 #edb/ref 1071 "The point $P$ with coordinates $(2,-1)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x)-3.$ Which of the following points must lie on $y=f(x)+1?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101355 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e2e3553c-e4d2-5b12-8a22-fe04a05189b4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101355 #edb/ref 1074 "**Method 1**\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)+1$ is obtained by translating the graph of $y=f(x)-3$ by $4$ units *up.* \n\nHence, if the point $(2,-1)$ lies on $y=f(x)-3$, then the point \n\n$$\n(2,-1+4)=(2,3)\n$$\n\nlies on $y=f(x)+1.$\n\n![](images/46/468f1e720d1a4834f1547b0cd2fcb8c9bff1e0342d2d98e33a62bbf9d473701a.png)\n\n**Method 2**\n\nAs $(2,-1)$ lies on $y=f(x)-3$, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2)-3 & =-1 \\\\ f(2) & =-1+3 \\\\ f(2) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if $x=2$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =f(2)+1 \\\\  & =2+1 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the point $(2,3)$ must lie on $y=f(x)+1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101638 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186101640 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101638 #edb/ref 1061 14720 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101638 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f72e4e92-9df5-551e-bf9d-c6db356cd3c2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101638 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186101945 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101639 #edb/ref 1061 14721 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101639 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4f45cbb5-b569-58b3-95eb-fbb98b10614a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101639 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186100847 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101640 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186101704 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101640 #edb/ref 1061 14722 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101640 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e7dee1eb-ff16-5f46-b872-2042f7c92dcc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101640 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186100848 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101641 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186101639 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101641 #edb/ref 1061 14723 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101641 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ba6cf136-6d59-5437-b6e3-d44b8ca10f7b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101641 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186100849 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101704 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186101705 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101704 #edb/ref 1061 18128 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101704 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0612808f-4124-5dbf-a60c-734f4636a504" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101704 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186100887 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101705 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186101641 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101705 #edb/ref 1061 18130 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101705 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f13d8d49-a65d-58fb-9ad0-f736406fedbb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101705 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186101972 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101945 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose that we have the graph of $y=f(x)$ shown below. What would the graph of $y=f(x)+2$ look like, and how does it relate to the original curve?\n\n![](images/1a/1a55ebd7e9dc656f3a2488debf14e63e655ed7548b76f63c26498f09d96d65ca.png)\n\nFor each point on the graph of $y=f(x)$ there is a corresponding point on the graph of $y=f(x)+2$ with the same $x$-value and a $y$-value that is two units greater. So let's create a table of points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Points on [math]y=f(x)[/math]} & \\text{Points on [math]y=f(x)+2[/math]} \\\\ (-2,0) & (-2,2) \\\\ (0,1.5) & (0,3.5) \\\\ (1,2) & (1,4) \\\\ (3,-3) & (3,-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvery point on the graph of $y=f(x)+2$ is precisely two units above a point on the graph of $y=f(x)$. The graph of $y=f(x)+2$ is an **upward vertical translation** of the graph of $y=f(x)$ by two units.\n\n![](images/66/6636d65acf9cd34d818d3d9e12a7b7de36dec4ceb70c7503a08751f971868855.png)\n\nOn the other hand, for each point on the graph of $y=f(x)$ there is a corresponding point on the graph of $y=f(x)-2$ with the same $x$-value and a $y$-value that is two units smaller. So let's create a table of points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Points on [math]y=f(x)[/math]} & \\text{Points on [math]y=f(x)-2[/math]} \\\\ (-2,0) & (-2,-2) \\\\ (0,1.5) & (0,-0.5) \\\\ (1,2) & (1,0) \\\\ (3,-3) & (3,-5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvery point on the graph of $y=f(x)-2$ is two units below a point on the graph of $y=f(x)$. The graph of $y=f(x)-2$ is a **downward vertical translation** of the graph of $y=f(x)$ by two units.\n\n![](images/72/72588aaa92f5b4bf9923c381cf5ad28ebb7288e77f6442549d0ffe1fdac3dc48.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101945 #edb/ref 1065 2809 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101945 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "198f4c8f-65df-5b93-951c-12fd4d9dfd12" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101945 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101972 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the solid curve $y=f(x)$ and the dashed curve $y=f(x)+b$ shown below. What is the value of $b?$\n\n![](images/b0/b058da1ea31e45f3029f1542642d802a35da7280f315a37ea92044e5c322fbd4.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)+b$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by translating it $6$ units down. So, $b=-6.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101972 #edb/ref 1065 3295 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101972 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "f2c0aba0-7e3b-5a75-83b0-7386793a015b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101972 #edb/ref 1067 "Identifying the Shift" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549310 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186550023 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549310 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186550036 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549310 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186550082 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549310 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549310 #edb/ref 1045 "Vertical Translations of Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549310 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186550036 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549310 #edb/ref 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046647 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549310 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050647 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549310 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "87827238-a8af-5c97-9258-aa69642e3f16" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549322 #edb/ref 1080 "-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549322 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549322 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "02