db/ref 17592186549707 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549836 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549836 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549836 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186549520 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549836 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "559dfa33-1ae3-585f-bec7-2a590aa14d78" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549967 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186549836 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549967 #edb/ref 1069 "q-26056" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549967 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549967 #edb/ref 1071 "The point $Q$ with coordinates $(-1,1)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x)$ and the point $R$ with coordinates $(-1,-2)$ lies on the curve $y=g(x).$ If the curve $y=g(x)$ is related to $y=f(x)$ via the transformation $g(x)=f(x)+k$, then what is the value of $k?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549967 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6bd4c5ad-d5d4-5f1c-b1a3-32f3fc367847" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549967 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that the point $R(-1,-2)$ lies $3$ units below the point $Q(-1,1).$ So, the graph of $y=g(x)$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by shifting it $3$ units down. \n\n![](images/7d/7d470919b374a5796b49ff81f893f37f5bb76afd4546a25459259f3b6339529b.png)\n\nAs a result, $k=-3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549975 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186549814 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549975 #edb/ref 1069 "q-86632" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549975 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549975 #edb/ref 1071 "The point $Q$ with coordinates $(-2,1)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x)$ and the point $R$ with coordinates $(-2,-1)$ lies on the curve $y=g(x).$ If the curve $y=g(x)$ is related to $y=f(x)$ via the transformation $g(x)=f(x)+k$, then what is the value of $k?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549975 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "841b4d8f-31c4-51b2-96fd-34cf02a240b8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549975 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that the point $R(-2,-1)$ lies $2$ units below the point $Q(-2,1).$ So, the graph of $y=g(x)$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by shifting it $2$ units down. \n\n![](images/15/158488a446217332d0b70d2e6b116bc1e17e7e5b205ff5ebe4289a75ef05fa05.png)\n\nAs a result, $k=-2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550023 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186550082 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550023 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "01e04307-8907-58c2-83c8-12dc65028b26" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550023 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186101355 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550036 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186550023 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550036 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "27c6e6f7-3931-5a76-88e4-d6e4ba6f3303" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550036 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186549967 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550082 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9f9b1109-0afb-53ad-9318-3e619c5a55d9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550082 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186549975 #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
